I can see the issue now. The file permissions are ---x--x--x, which means:

Owner (root): --x (execute only — no read, no write)

Group: --x (execute only)

Others: --x (execute only)

The problem is that no one has READ permission on the file. Even though the execute bit is set, a shell script needs to be readable by the user running it (or by the shell/interpreter) to actually execute.

When you run bash /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh, bash tries to read the file — but it can't, hence "Permission denied."

🔧 The Fix
You need to add read permission for all users (or at least give read + execute):

bash
sudo chmod +rx /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
This adds read (r) and execute (x) for all users. The result will be:

text
-r-xr-xr-x 1 root root 40 Oct  8 19:33 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
Or, if you want to match the typical expected permission set (rwxr-xr-x):

bash
sudo chmod 755 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
Why chmod +x didn't work
chmod +x only adds the execute bit. Since the file already had x for all users, the command had no effect — it didn't touch the missing read bits.

✅ Verify and Test
bash
ls -l /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
cat /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
/tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
After the fix, you should see something like:

text
-r-xr-xr-x 1 root root 40 Oct  8 19:33 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
And the script should execute without errors.

📝 Summary
Command	Effect
chmod +x	Adds execute only (no effect if x already present)
chmod +rx	Adds read and execute for all users ✅
chmod 755	Sets rwxr-xr-x — full standard permissions ✅
chmod 555	Sets r-xr-xr-x — read+execute for all, no write
Run this to complete the task:

bash
sudo chmod 755 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
